SwiftPutty local patch 0022
Upstream: PuTTY 0.85 (a3cce3a48d4ab12e0fccb32998b9042d4f7c3ff1)
Status:   not yet reported upstream; suitable for upstream

fd sockets: a write to a command that has exited fails with EPIPE
instead of raising SIGPIPE.

Network sockets are written with MSG_NOSIGNAL, but fd-socket.c writes
to the pipes of local proxy commands, pre-connect commands and
authentication plugins with plain write(). If the command exited while
data was still being sent, the process got SIGPIPE; unless the program
ignores it, as plink does, that ends the process. In SwiftPutty, an app
with every session in one process, one proxy command exiting early
would close every window. Where the platform has it (macOS
F_SETNOSIGPIPE), the output descriptor is now set not to raise SIGPIPE,
so the existing EPIPE handling reports the error on that connection.

Found by Core/tests/spb_session_test.c (a local proxy command that stops
reading, with SIGPIPE at its default disposition).

--- a/unix/fd-socket.c
+++ b/unix/fd-socket.c
@@ -347,6 +347,17 @@
     fds->outfd = outfd;
     fds->inerrfd = inerrfd;
 
+#ifdef F_SETNOSIGPIPE
+    /*
+     * A write to a pipe whose reader has gone (a proxy command or
+     * plugin that exited) should fail with EPIPE, which is handled
+     * below, rather than raise SIGPIPE and end the whole process.
+     * (Network sockets get the same effect from MSG_NOSIGNAL.)
+     */
+    if (fds->outfd >= 0)
+        fcntl(fds->outfd, F_SETNOSIGPIPE, 1);
+#endif
+
     if (fds->outfd >= 0) {
         if (!fdsocket_by_outfd)
             fdsocket_by_outfd = newtree234(fdsocket_outfd_cmp);
